Title: The Load Path
Author: Audit Fixture

/*
PROVENANCE. Original work written for this repository as a test fixture, 2026-09-04,
during the advice-quality audit (docs/scoring/ADVICE_RULE_FIXES_2026-09-04.md). Not
derived from any published screenplay. Dedicated to the public domain under CC0 1.0.

ROLE. The NEGATIVE fixture of a matched pair. Deliberately well-made at 10 scenes /
~1,139 words: a real midpoint reversal (the weld is fine, the bearing pad is not), an
active protagonist who chooses the costly option on the page, planted objects that pay
off (the green folder, the fatigue page, the red tag), and dialogue that works by
refusal and subtext rather than statement. Its partner bad.fountain is matched to it on
scene count and length so that anything that separates them is craft, not size.

Lines this pair exists to protect, each the site of a measured false positive:
  * TESS: "You're asking me a question you already know the answer to."  -- a REFUSAL
    of exposition, once reported as AS_YOU_KNOW_BOB (major).
  * NOOR: "Yes."  -- an admission that ends her career, once reported as
    SYCOPHANTIC_AGREEMENT, "no conflict or subtext".
  * "She runs a thumbnail along it" / "the drone shot before the light goes flat"  --
    a thumbnail and a CAMERA shot, once the two danger-lexicon hits that made scene 1
    the peak-suspense scene of a script that climaxes at scene 9.
  * "Checks the time: 8:52."  -- once reported as a colon used as a dramatic-reveal
    device.
Do not "improve" any of those lines; each is load-bearing for a specific regression.
*/

EXT. HARBOR FOOTBRIDGE - CONSTRUCTION SITE - DAWN

A new pedestrian span, seventy meters of white steel, still wearing its scaffold like a coat it hasn't taken off. Bunting is being strung at the east abutment.

NOOR HAMDAN, 31, hard hat under her arm, crouches at a weld seam with a pocket light. She runs a thumbnail along it.

GIL ABARA, 60s, watches her from the ramp with a thermos he isn't drinking from.

GIL
Ribbon's at eleven. The mayor's people want the drone shot before the light goes flat.

NOOR
There's a hairline at the third splice.

GIL
There's a hairline at every third splice on every bridge in this city. You are the most careful engineer I have ever trained.

NOOR
You say that the way other people say slow.

GIL
I say it the way people say expensive.

He sets the thermos down on the green folder tucked under his arm, and picks the folder back up before she can look at it.

INT. SITE TRAILER - MORNING

Drawings papered over the windows. Noor pulls the green folder from the shelf and opens it to the fatigue-check page.

Her own signature sits at the bottom of it. Dated Friday.

She holds the page up to the window light, then photographs it with her phone. Then she photographs the sign-in sheet on the wall beside it: Friday, one name, one time out. GIL ABARA, 9:40 PM.

INT. SITE TRAILER - CORRIDOR - CONTINUOUS

TESS OKONJO, 40s, foreman, is filling a jug at the cooler. Noor stops beside her and does not show her the phone.

NOOR
Friday night. Who locked up?

TESS
You're asking me a question you already know the answer to.

NOOR
I'm asking you whether you want to be the one who said it.

Tess screws the cap on slowly.

TESS
He was here till ten. He had the folder open on the table and he had a pen in his hand and I walked past twice and both times I decided I was thirsty.

She lifts the jug.

TESS (CONT'D)
I'm still thirsty.

INT. GIL'S OFFICE - SITE TRAILER - DAY

Noor puts the fatigue page on the desk between them, face up. Gil does not pick it up.

GIL
It's a good signature. I've watched you make it four hundred times.

NOOR
That's not a denial.

GIL
No.

NOOR
Say the rest of it.

GIL
You would have signed it. Monday, Tuesday, whenever you got to it. All I did was move the date.

NOOR
You moved my name.

GIL
I moved a date.

NOOR
Then it won't bother you when the city inspector reads it at eleven.

She takes the page back. Gil finally looks up, and what's in his face isn't fear. It's something closer to disappointment that she's making him watch.

EXT. SITE - EMPLOYEE LOT - DAY

Noor, phone to her ear, walking fast between pickups.

NOOR
(into phone)
This is Noor Hamdan, HB Structural, for Inspector Reyes. It's about the harbor span sign-off. I need him before the ceremony, not after.

She hangs up. Checks the time: 8:52. Looks at the bridge. Then opens the truck's tailgate, drags out a strain gauge kit, and slings it over her shoulder.

EXT. HARBOR FOOTBRIDGE - UNDERSIDE - DAY

Noor, harnessed, working out along the beam soffit in the dark under the deck. She clamps the gauge at the third splice, the one she flagged.

The readout settles. She stares at it.

NOOR
(to herself)
No.

She unclamps, moves two meters east, reclamps. Same number. Good number. The weld is fine. The weld was always fine.

She lets her light drift down, off the splice, onto the bearing pad at the pier head. Neoprene, seated, ordinary.

She goes very still.

She pulls her phone, opens a photo of a calc sheet in her own handwriting, and holds it up beside the pad. Her lips move through the arithmetic once. Twice.

NOOR (CONT'D)
(quietly)
That's mine.

INT. SITE TRAILER - DAY

Gil is at the window with his back to the door when she comes in. She doesn't sit.

NOOR
The bearing pad at pier two is sized for a service load I got wrong in March. You caught it.

GIL
I caught it.

NOOR
And instead of telling me, you signed the fatigue page yourself so the packet that goes in the city file has my name on the part I got right and your handwriting on nothing.

GIL
The pad will hold. Twenty years, thirty. It'll be replaced in a maintenance cycle by someone who never knows.

NOOR
That isn't what I asked.

Gil turns around.

GIL
I have eleven months left on my license and a granddaughter who thinks I build things. I wanted to hand you a practice with no mistake in it.

NOOR
You wanted to hand me a practice with your mistake in it instead of mine. That's not a gift, Gil. That's a mortgage.

EXT. EAST ABUTMENT - RIBBON PLATFORM - DAY

Folding chairs. A ribbon. A press photographer testing the light. INSPECTOR REYES, 50s, arrives with a clipboard and shakes hands he doesn't care about.

Tess stands at the rail with the crowd, watching Noor come up the ramp with the green folder under her arm.

Noor stops. Looks at the ribbon. Looks at the span, which is beautiful, and which will hold, probably, for thirty years.

She keeps walking.

EXT. HARBOR FOOTBRIDGE - MIDSPAN - DAY

Noor steps over the ribbon, out onto the deck, and turns to face the chairs. The photographer, delighted, starts shooting.

NOOR
The load rating on this span is provisional. Pier two's bearing is undersized against the design service load, by my calculation, in March, with my name on it.

A ripple through the chairs. Reyes lowers the clipboard.

NOOR (CONT'D)
It'll open. It'll open in six weeks with a pad that's right.

She takes out the red tag from her back pocket, hangs it on the rail, and signs it — slowly, all the way through, the way you sign something you intend to be found.

INT. SITE TRAILER - LATE AFTERNOON

Empty chairs visible through the window. Gil is packing a box, badly.

GIL
Reyes will pull the fatigue page. Handwriting people will look at it.

NOOR
Yes.

GIL
You could have let it go until Monday and then quietly fixed the pad.

NOOR
I know.

GIL
Why didn't you?

Noor opens the green folder on the desk, turns to the fatigue page, and draws a single line through the signature at the bottom.

Then she signs it again underneath, today's date, her own hand.

NOOR
Because I want the file to say what happened.

She closes the folder and leaves it on his desk, open, facing him.
